    "body": "> > The alternative here is adding a Node.js or Python step to the build process which post-processes ahead of time (since none of us want to write a syntax highlighter in Free Pascal).\r\n>\r\n> I've got some decent code for Python already in Bikeshed, using the pretty-good Pygments library. It properly preserves markup in the original code, too, which a lot of highlighters don't do.\r\n\r\nSeems like their would be clear value in sharing code with Bikeshed on the syntax highlighting, and I’m not opposed to having some Python processing added to the build process to get it.\r\n\r\nI think in fact that might be better than writing it into wattsi, because it will be easier to maintain. Even when we can manage to implement stuff up in the wattsi code, it comes at the cost of having a very small pool of people able/interested in maintaining it.\r\n\r\nSo I like the idea of doing this in Python with the Bikeshed code.\r\n\r\nThe only thing blocking that is somebody stepping forward to actually implement it vs what we have at hand now that we can land pretty easily and that gets the feature out to users sooner rather than delaying it further.\r\n\r\n> But going with a runtime version seems OK for now, as long as it doesn't jank singlepage too much. (And even that could probably be fixed upstream with judicious requestIdleCallback/fallback-to-setTimeout usage.)\r\n\r\nYeah, I would prefer we get something out to users sooner rather than later, and going forward would be willing to put time myself into implementing a replacement in Python. I just can’t do it any time soon (not this month nor next—and come August, my free cycles will be going way down for several months, which is part of the reason I am putting time now into trying to get some of these features landed in some form now).",
